Default O_NONBLOCK to 0 where the platform lacks it

cctz's zoneinfo reader takes the POSIX branch on mingw, since that is guarded on
_MSC_VER, and opens the file with O_NONBLOCK, which mingw-w64 does not define:
there is no such notion in the Win32 API. Default it to 0 the way the very next
lines already do for O_CLOEXEC. The guard that actually matters, the S_ISREG
check on the opened descriptor, is unaffected, and Windows has no POSIX FIFO for
the non-blocking open to protect against in the first place.

diff --git a/absl/time/internal/cctz/src/time_zone_info.cc b/absl/time/internal/cctz/src/time_zone_info.cc
--- a/absl/time/internal/cctz/src/time_zone_info.cc
+++ b/absl/time/internal/cctz/src/time_zone_info.cc
@@ -413,6 +413,9 @@ inline FilePtr FOpen(const char* path) {
 #ifndef O_CLOEXEC
 #define O_CLOEXEC 0
 #endif
+#ifndef O_NONBLOCK
+#define O_NONBLOCK 0
+#endif
   const int fd = open(path, O_RDONLY | O_NONBLOCK | O_CLOEXEC);
   if (fd >= 0) {
     struct stat st;
